findings.body:
**Export `PlannerUsageError` from `runner.ts`. Without it, the `crq` CLI cannot load.**

`src/queue-runner/tick.ts:58` imports `PlannerUsageError` as a value from `./runner.js`, and `tick.ts:389` throws it. `runner.ts` does not declare or export this name, and no file under `src` declares it. `package.json` sets `"type": "module"`, so the missing named import fails at module link time. `crq.ts` statically imports `planTick` from `./tick.js`. The `crq` entry (`dist/queue-runner/crq.js`) therefore fails before any command runs, including `watchdog`. TypeScript reports the same missing exported member.

Until the export exists, `planTick` never runs, so `crq tick --dry-run --unexpected` does not reach the catch at `runner.ts:139-142`.

After the class is exported, that catch still returns exit code 1 for this error. Map `PlannerUsageError` to the existing usage artifact with exit code 2. Doctrine I1 requires exit code 2 and a usage artifact for argument errors.

<details>
<summary>Suggested fix</summary>

Add the class beside the exported types in `runner.ts`. Its constructor matches the single message argument at `tick.ts:389`:

```diff
+export class PlannerUsageError extends Error {
+  constructor(message: string) {
+    super(message);
+    this.name = 'PlannerUsageError';
+  }
+}
+
 /** Planners describe effects; only the dry-run recorder is implemented in this skeleton. */
 export async function run(
```

Return the usage artifact from the dry-run catch:

```diff
   } catch (error) {
+    if (error instanceof PlannerUsageError) return usage(error.message);
     return {
       artifact: { status: 'failed', error: error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error) },
       exitCode: 1,
```

</details>
